{"type": "thinking", "text": "We need solve 4 problems. Need at least 2. Let's think carefully. This is a hard task. We have plenty time 3000s. We can implement all 4 perhaps.\n\nLet's analyze each.\n\n### abc387_f Count Arrays\n\nGiven functional graph A: i -> A_i. Conditions x_i <= x_{A_i}. Count assignments in [1,M] mod 998244353. N,M <=2025.\n\nFunctional graph decomposes into components each with one cycle, with trees feeding into cycle. Condition along edges parent value >= child value. Need count.\n\nFor each tree rooted at cycle node, given root value v? We can DP. Number of assignments on tree where all x_child <= parent and values in 1..M. Standard DP can compute based on root value using polynomial? Need efficient O(N M?) Maybe use dp for each node as function of its value: f_u(v)=number assignments subtree rooted at u with x_u=v and constraints, = product over children (sum_{w<=v} f_child(w))? Wait constraints x_child <= x_parent=v, so child root can be any w <= v, and its subtree. Let g_child(v)=sum_{w=1}^v f_child(w). Then f_u(v)=prod children g_child(v). For cycle edges, x_i <= x_{A_i}, cyclic inequalities: around cycle all x equal (since x_i <= x_next ... cycling means all equal). Thus for each cycle, all x values must be equal to some v. Then count for cycle with attached trees: product over cycle nodes f_u(v) (where f_u includes off-cycle children only, with root u at v). Sum over v=1..M. For tree nodes, compute f_u(v) arrays length M and prefix. Naive tree edges O(NM) if each child combine pointwise; fine N*M ~4M.\n\nBut root cycle nodes also have maybe tree children (not cycle next). Compute f for all rooted trees excluding cycle edge. Need process reverse topological order from leaves. Since functional graph, work by peeling indegree or DFS. Simpler: detect cycles. For each node, build reverse adjacency excluding cycle edges? Actually each node has one outgoing to parent; children are reverse. Cycle nodes depending on direction: edge i -> A_i means constraint x_i<=x_{A_i}, so parent A_i, children list. We need process non-cycle nodes in order of distance to cycle. Kahn peeling: indegrees on graph i->A_i, leaves indeg 0. Then process leaf to parent accumulating children product prefix. But combining child prefix pointwise can be done when child finalized: f_parent(v) initially 1; multiply by prefix(child)(v). We can accumulate modulo; O(NM). Then cycle nodes: their f arrays include all non-cycle children? In Kahn, when child processed, multiply into parent. But cycle node also has a cycle child edge not processed (since cycle indegree >=1). So f_cycle after Kahn = product over non-cycle children only (excluding cycle predecessor). Great. For each cycle, sum_v product_{cycle u} f_u(v). Multiply component counts. Note cycles of length 1: self-loop A_i=i. Constraint x_i<=x_i trivial. Cycle of length1 product f_i(v) and sum v. Fine.\n\nKahn algorithm: initialize f[u][v]=1 for v=1..M for all nodes. indeg. Queue nodes indeg 0 (not on cycles). Pop u; compute prefix g of f[u]; then for parent p=A[u], f[p][v] *= g[v] pointwise. decrement indeg[p]; if 0 queue p. At end nodes with indeg>0 are cycles. Then cycle count. Complexity O(NM), memory f N*(M+1) ~2025*2026 Python ints ~ maybe 4M Python ints ~ 112MB plus lists overhead ~ okay maybe memory limit unknown but typically 1024MB. Could optimize arrays but okay. Need prefix each node. Could store f as list of lists. 4M ints potentially 112MB plus list overhead 32MB, around 150MB. AtCoder memory likely 1024 MiB. But hidden judge maybe. We can use list of lists. Time O(NM) ~4M multiplications, fine.\n\nWait processing each node computing prefix O(M) and multiply into parent O(M), total O(NM) ~8M. Fine.\n\nSample check later.\n\n### abc388_f Dangerous Sugoroku\n\nN up to 1e12, M<=2e4 intervals bad, A,B<=20. Need reachability along line with allowed jumps d in [A,B] to good cells. Bad cells are intervals within (1,N). Need determine if N reachable.\n\nThis is known ABC388 F solution using DP over intervals with small A,B and coordinate compression; maintain reachable states near boundaries. Since steps max B<=20. Need handle huge gaps. Bad intervals are obstacles; can't land on bad cells but can jump over them. Movement strictly forward.\n\nWe can process good segments and use bitset of reachable positions modulo? Because max step 20, reachability eventually periodic/stable. N huge but obstacles finite.\n\nApproach: DP positions reachable. Good cells outside bad intervals. For a long run of consecutive good cells, if there's any reachable window pattern, eventually all positions become reachable (if A<=B). Indeed with step interval [A,B], if consecutive good positions length long enough, reachable set can stabilize. We can process interval by interval using bitset and some cycle detection due to B<=20? But A maybe >1, patterns can grow eventually. We can model reachable statuses over last B positions as bitmask; transition over a good cell shifts? For each next position i, reachable iff cell good and exists reachable j in [i-B, i-A]. This depends on previous B reachable bits. State is B-bit mask of reachability of last B positions. For long stretches of good cells, state evolves deterministically. B<=20, state space 2^20 ~1M, could detect cycle but might take 1M per gap, and 2e4 gaps => too much. But actually dynamics may converge quickly or cycle small. We can maybe exploit structure.\n\nAlternative known solution: because A,B <=20, for huge N, DP only near each interval plus perhaps compress long good ranges to length ~ something. If there is a good segment of length >= some threshold (like 2B or function), the reachable pattern becomes all ones? Let's examine. Suppose from some reachable position x, can we reach all sufficiently far good positions? With step [A,B], gcd maybe g = gcd of interval? Actually set of step lengths includes consecutive values A..B. If A<=B. From position, can move any amount between A and B. If all cells good for a long stretch, reachable set likely fills entire tail beyond some point. Because from one reachable position p, positions p+A..p+B reachable, then next etc. Does it fill contiguous from some point? Let's test A=3,B=5: reachable from 0 (start): [3,5], then [6,10] maybe all? 6..10 yes (3+3..5+5), then 9..15, union => 3.. continuously? Overlaps because 2A=6 <= B+1? For consecutive intervals. But initially gaps maybe. In general after some steps, all positions beyond threshold become reachable if gcd(A..B)=1? gcd(A,B) may be >1, e.g., A=B=2, reachable parity fixed. So not all. But states are periodic with gcd g = gcd of all d? Actually allowed steps A..B, gcd of set = gcd(A, A+1,...,B). For B>A, gcd=1 always (consecutive integers). If A<B, gcd=1. If A=B, only step A, reachable positions congruent to start mod A. Important. So case A=B can handle separately: only move exactly A; bad intervals; reachable positions are 1 + kA, need N ≡1 mod A and no bad at those positions. M intervals cover along line. Easy check.\n\nIf A<B, gcd=1, then over a long all-good run, reachability eventually becomes contiguous all positions (after some bounded length). Need bound. We can just simulate up to say length 2*B^2? Or find theoretical threshold to compress. Since B<=20, we can simulate long good gaps up to some bound maybe 10000 and then once all last B positions reachable, all future are reachable (because union of [i-B,i-A] covers all previous, if all previous B reachable then next reachable; once B consecutive reachable, every next good is reachable). Actually if all positions in [i-B,i-1] reachable, then i reachable via step A (or any) since i-A in range and A<=B; also need i-A >= i-B yes. So absorbing all-ones state. We can simulate each long good segment and stop early when last B all reachable. Number of steps to absorbing? Could be potentially large? State space 2^B=1M; but likely small. We can analyze worst-case maybe not huge but with 2e4 gaps could be up to 1M*2e4 bad. But can prove for A<B absorbing within O(B^2/g?) Let's think.\n\nReachable set from a contiguous block of reachable positions of length L. If we have a run of B consecutive reachable positions, then all future reachable. Starting from any reachable position, can we generate B consecutive reachable within some steps? With allowed [A,B], A<B. Starting from one reachable p. After one move reachable interval [p+A,p+B] length B-A+1 consecutive. If B-A+1 maybe small. After t moves, reachable positions from p are all sums of t steps, range [tA, tB]. Because steps can vary but sums may have gaps? With t steps of integers in [A,B], any integer between tA and tB is achievable (since choose t numbers summing to S for any S in range). Yes. Thus after t moves from p, all positions in [p+tA, p+tB] are reachable (provided all in good segment). The interval length t(B-A)+1 grows. We need get B consecutive reachable. Since each t interval is consecutive, when t(B-A)+1 >= B, i.e., t >= (B-1)/(B-A), we get B consecutive. But also intervals across different t; just the t-th move set already B consecutive. So after ceil((B-1)/(B-A)) steps, all future good positions reachable. For A<B, B-A>=1, t<=B-1 <=19. So within 20 steps after a reachable point in a long good segment, absorbing. Nice. Even if A=B, interval length 1, never.\n\nThus for A<B, process good gaps: if gap length large, simulate maybe up to B steps after first reachable then can mark all future positions reachable. But need careful across bad intervals: reachability at positions after bad interval depends on reachable positions before it within B distance. We can process relevant cells around bad intervals, and for long good runs use compression.\n\nSimplest: coordinate compress DP over \"events\" with gaps. Since B small, we can simulate each good cell in segments of length maybe <= some threshold, and for longer segments, after at most B-1 steps of having a reachable point, all cells from some point onward in that seg