n\nWe'll test sample.\n\n### abc389_g Odd Even Graph\n\nThis is hardest. Need count connected simple labeled graphs on N vertices with M edges, N even, such that number of vertices at even distance from 1 equals number at odd distance. N<=30, P prime 1e8..1e9 (may not fit? P <=1e9, multiplication of mod values up to 1e18 fits Python anyway). Need output for all M from N-1 to N(N-1)/2.\n\nNeed derive combinatorial DP.\n\nWe need count connected graphs with a specified bipartition by BFS layers parity? Condition: BFS color from 1 has equal sizes of even/odd distance vertices. Note graph not necessarily bipartite. Distance parity coloring: even-distance vertices E, odd-distance O. For any edge, distance parity can be same or differ; but if edge connects two vertices of same parity, there might be shorter odd cycle etc. Important property: In BFS from root 1, for an edge (u,v), |dist(u)-dist(v)|<=1. Thus no edge between vertices whose distances differ >1. Parity classes: edges within same parity connect vertices of equal distance or distance 2; edges across parity connect distances differing 1 (cannot connect same distance because same distance parity same? across parity means distances parity differ, difference must be 1). So graph structure layered by distance but we only track parity perhaps not enough because edges within same parity can connect distance differing 2, which affects connectivity? Condition only counts parity. We might count all connected graphs where BFS parity classes have equal size; need enumerate.\n\nN<=30 suggests DP over subsets? 2^30 too large. Maybe exploit that number of vertices at odd/even distance must equal, so N/2 each, but labels: choose which vertices are odd side? Wait graph has labeled root 1, even side includes root, total N/2 even, N/2 odd. We can choose labels for odd side: C(N-1, N/2) perhaps. But property of BFS distance parity imposes that every vertex in odd side has at least one edge to even side at distance one less, and every even non-root vertex has at least one edge to odd side at distance one less; more importantly adjacency constraints by BFS layers. Counting via partitions by parity may not directly enforce BFS distances because edges within sides can create shortcuts.\n\nMaybe there is a clever characterization: For a connected graph with root 1, define even/odd by shortest path parity. The condition equal counts. Could count connected bipartite-like graphs? Let's examine structure when we contract? Graph may have odd cycles; then same parity edges exist.\n\nI recall this problem ABC389 G \"Odd Even Graph\" perhaps solution uses exponential generating functions / graph counting with BFS layers and total N=30, O(N^4). Maybe all connected graphs satisfying condition can be counted by choosing a bipartition and using inclusion-exclusion? Let's search memory: AtCoder ABC389 G editorial maybe uses \"number of connected graphs with equal even/odd BFS vertices\" and formula via chromatic? Hmm.\n\nLet's reason deeply.\n\nWe need count connected simple graphs with M edges and equal parity layers. N even <=30. All graphs connected on N labeled vertices. Root fixed 1. Condition based on BFS distances.\n\nCould use the fact that BFS parity classes form a \"bipartition\" where all vertices have correct parity if we assign levels. Counting labelings with BFS may be done by considering a partition of vertices into ordered layers V_0={1}, V_1, V_2,... (nonempty until depth D). Edges allowed:\n- Every vertex in V_k (k>=1) has at least one edge to V_{k-1}.\n- No edges between V_i and V_j with |i-j|>1.\n- Edges within same layer V_k allowed (these are same parity, distance equal).\n- Edges between V_i and V_{i+1} arbitrary except coverage.\n- For BFS exact distances, requiring no edges skipping more than one layer and coverage of previous layer is sufficient? Yes, if layers are actual BFS distances: vertices in V_k adjacent only to V_{k-1},V_k,V_{k+1}, and each has neighbor in V_{k-1}; then shortest distance exactly k. Edges within V_k don't change distances. So any graph with a valid layered structure yields those BFS layers.\n\nBut counting over all possible layer partitions and sizes summing N, with equal total even/odd sizes, can be done by summing over ordered compositions of N with each part >=1 (except V0=1), and for each, count edge sets satisfying coverage. Number of compositions 2^{N-1} ~5e8 for 30 too large, but DP over total vertices and parity counts, plus number of edges, can aggregate because edge count combinatorics depends only on consecutive layer sizes and within-layer sizes, not all layers? Coverage for each layer depends only on previous and current layer sizes (number of bipartite graphs between them with every vertex in current layer having degree>=1 to previous). Edges within layer and to next handled similarly. So we can build DP over layers with state current layer size, accumulated even/odd counts, accumulated edges, and maybe previous layer size. Since edge count between V_{k-1},V_k and within V_k can be accounted when adding V_k, and coverage of V_k to V_{k-1}. The next layer edges will be accounted later. This seems feasible O(N^6 M) maybe N=30.\n\nBut careful: edges within V_k and between V_{k-1},V_k can be chosen independently. The requirement each vertex in V_k has at least one neighbor in V_{k-1}; edges within V_k arbitrary; edges from V_{k-1} to V_k also constrained no? For BFS, vertices in V_{k-1} can have edges to V_k, no further requirement. So when adding layer of size c after previous size p (k>=1), the number of ways to choose edges between previous and current such that every current vertex has at least one neighbor in previous is:\nW(p,c) = sum_{j=0}^{c} (-1)^j C(c,j) 2^{p(c-j)}? Actually choose subset of current vertices that have no neighbor to previous; inclusion-exclusion: ∑_{i=0}^c (-1)^i C(c,i) 2^{p(c-i)}. Equiv number of c-tuples of nonempty subsets of p = (2^p-1)^c? Wait each current vertex chooses a nonempty subset of previous vertices as neighbors; edges determined by choices, and multiple current vertices independent. Number = (2^p-1)^c. Yes simple! Because for each current vertex, its neighbor set in previous must be nonempty: 2^p -1 choices. No other constraints. Great. So coverage factor = (2^p -1)^c.\n\nThen edges within current layer: choose e among C(c,2) arbitrary: C(C(c,2), e). Edges between previous and current already counted in choices? Wait (2^p-1)^c counts exact neighbor set for each current vertex, which determines all p*c potential edges but only those incident to current; each edge has one endpoint in current (except previous-previous already decided). Since each current vertex has nonempty subset, number of possible bipartite edge sets with no isolated vertex on current side is indeed product over current vertices (2^p-1), and this includes all possible edge counts with a generating function not uniform. To track total edges we need distribution by number of edges between layers, not just total count factor. We need count bipartite graphs between p and c with every current vertex degree >=1, by number of edges. Then combine with within-layer edges.\n\nSo define B[p][c][e] = number of bipartite graphs with p vertices left, c right, every right vertex has degree>=1, and e edges, mod P. Need compute for p,c<=30, e<=p*c. Could use inclusion-exclusion over right vertices:\nB[p][c][e] = ∑_{i=0}^c (-1)^i C(c,i) * C(p*(c-i), e) (choose i right vertices isolated; remaining arbitrary edges) - but inclusion-exclusion counts graphs where at least all? Yes number with no isolated right vertices = ∑_{i=0}^c (-1)^i C(c,i) * C(p(c-i), e). Here choose i specific right vertices to be isolated, remaining edges arbitrary among p*(c-i). Correct. Precompute binomial up to C(900, e) mod P. P prime but may be small? P>=1e8, N(N-1)/2<=435, p*c<=225? Actually total potential edges <=435. P>435 so denominators okay if using factorials, but P may less than? P>=1e8 >900, safe. We can precompute binom via Pascal mod P up to 435 maybe. But C(p*(c-i), e) up to 435, can use Pascal or factorial inverses. Since P prime > 1e8, factorial inverses safe. Or Pascal O(435^2) fine.\n\nThen DP over layers. Need total even/odd counts equal N/2 each. Layer 0 size1 even. Subsequent layers alternate parity. We need count connected graphs with BFS layers arbitrary length, each layer nonempty? By BFS definition layers for k up to D nonempty, then no vertices. We sum over all possible layer sizes positive. Each graph has unique BFS layer partition, so no double count.\n\nDP state: after processing layers up to k, total vertices used t (including root), total even count e, total odd count o (t=e+o), total edges m accumulated from all within-layer edges and between layers up to k. Also previous layer size p and parity of next layer. We can transition add next layer size c>=1 if t+c<=N. Parity of new layer is (k+1)%2; since k determined? If we track parity toggling. DP maybe process layers with index and state (e,o,last_size,m) and next parity. Since e+o=t, and layer parity can be inferred by whether next layer is even or odd. Could just track number of layers? Easier: DP over sequence of layer sizes with alternating colors. Since layer0 even size1. We add layers; maintain even count e and odd count o. We can store dp[last_size][e][o][m]? But after each added layer, last_size c; colors determined by layer index not needed if we know e,o and starting even? Next layer parity = opposite of last layer parity, but need know last parity. Since layers alternate, can track parity or simply note next color alternates; we can keep two arrays depending on index? Or state include next parity. Since total even/odd count, and root even, layer indices parity known by number of layers, but we can just use a dimension for parity of last added layer (0 even,1 odd); next is 1-parity. Or process separately with layer count k. Complexity N layers up to N, each state last_size up to N, e up to N/2, o up to N/2, m up to 435; transitions c up to N. That could be huge: N * N * (N/2)^2 * M * N ~ 30*30*225*435*30 ~ 2.6e9 impossible.\n\nNeed optimize. We can aggregate t and perhaps e,o constrained equal at end. Maybe use generating polynomial in x for vertex color and y for edges. We only need final e=o=N/2. Could track difference d=e-o rather than both and t. State: t total vertices, d=e-o, m edges, last_size p, last_parity. d range [-N,N]. That's N*N*(2N)*M*N ~ 30*30*60*435*30 ~ 705M maybe high in Python but maybe reduce transitions.\n\nWait transitions c sizes, edge distributions B[p][c][*] and within-layer C(C(c,2),*) convolution. Complexity potentially huge. Need find better combinatorial approach exploiting N=30.\n\nMaybe there is a known more elegant formula. Let's think.\n\nAlternative characterize by the bipartition of BFS parity. Maybe count via selecting odd set O size N/2 and ensuring distances? We could use inclusion-exclusion / connected graph counts with constraints on adjacency between sets.\n\nObservation: Given a partition of vertices into E (contains 1) and O, when does there exist a graph such that BFS parity classes equal E,O? Conditions:\n- Every vertex in O must have a neighbor in E with smaller distance, but not directly can characterize without levels.\nMaybe all vertices in O are at odd distance and E even. An equivalent condition for a connected graph with root1: E is set of even-distance vertices iff:\n  1. No edge within O? Wait odd-odd edge connects two odd levels possibly same/diff 0 or2, allowed. No, BFS parity condition imposes not arbitrary.\n\nColor by parity of distance. Along any edge, distances differ at most1. Thus:\n- If edge connects E-E: their distances differ 0 or 2 (same parity). Could be within same level or skipping one level.\n- O-O similarly.\n- E-O: distances differ 1 exactly.\nBut can we characterize using \"no edge between vertices whose ...\"? The parity coloring is not necessarily a proper 2-coloring because within same color edges allowed. However, there is a retrograde property.\n\nMaybe use BFS distance parity and the fact that adding edges within same parity doesn't change distances if base connected? Could count via connected bipartite backbone plus extra edges within sides? Let's explore.\n\nSuppose we take a connected bipartite graph with parts E,O and root in E. BFS distances parity is correct (all E even, O odd) because graph bipartite. Then add any edges within E or within O. Does BFS parity remain same? Not always: adding an edge within same part creates odd cycle, which can shorten paths and flip parity of some vertices. Example path 1(E)-2(O)-3(E), add edge 1-3 within E; distances same parity 0,2 still even. In bipartite graph, any same-part edge connects vertices whose distance is even; but there may be a path length 2? Adding edge creates alternate path of length dist(u)+1+dist(v)? Could create shorter path of opposite parity? For vertices beyond, maybe parity flips if odd cycle accessible. Example E-O-E-O path, add edge between two O vertices at distances1 and3: edge makes distance from root to the level3 O become? root1 - O1 (1), O1-O3 edge -> O3 distance2 even, flips. So extra same-part edges can change parity. Thus not simple.\n\nMaybe root parity classes correspond to a \"bipartition\" after deleting same-parity edges? Odd cycles affect.\n\nCould count using BFS layers DP but need optimize for N=30. Maybe intended complexity O(N^5) with DP over layers and counts. Let's search memory of ABC389 G. I vaguely remember an editorial formula involving \"connected components of bipartite graph\" and exponential generating functions, maybe uses subset convolution over N? N=30, could use subset DP 2^{N/2}? Labels? Hmm N=30 suggests O(N^5) rather than 2^N maybe; but 2^N not feasible.\n\nLet's derive layer DP more carefully and estimate feasible optimized implementation. We can perhaps do DP over layers where each layer's contribution polynomial can be precomputed; number of layers ≤ N but most states may be sparse. Also we only need final e=N/2, o=N/2. We can use polynomial in vertex count and edge count and difference.\n\nLet's formulate with ordinary generating functions. We process layers. State after adding layer k: (p = size of last layer, t total vertices, d=e-o, m edges). But t and d determine e,o: e=(t+d)/2, o=(t-d)/2. Need e,o <= N/2 eventually? Actually we need exactly e=o=N/2; we can prune e>N/2 or o>N/2 because counts never decrease. This reduces states somewhat. Also t up to N.\n\nTransitions: choose c new vertices from remaining color? Wait vertices are labeled. We are counting ordered partitions of labels into layers. We process layer sizes but also need account for label assignments: For a sequence of sizes (1,c1,c2,...), number of ways to assign labels to layers = multinomial N! / (1! c1! c2! ...). Then for each assignment, graph choices independent based on sizes. We can incorporate factors via exponential generating functions: when adding a layer of size c of a given color, multiply by C(remaining of that color, c) perhaps. Since final color groups sizes fixed N/2 each, labels of E and O are predetermined sizes. We can assign labels incrementally: root fixed 1 in E. Then for each new even layer choose c labels from remaining even vertices; odd layer choose c from odd vertices. At end all used. Number of labelings automatically C(N/2-1, c_even layers...) * C(N/2, c_odd...). So transition factor for new layer: C(E_remaining, c) if color even, C(O_remaining, c) if odd. This depends on e and o (remaining = N/2 - e). But if we eventually need equal counts, we can restrict target sizes N/2. Yes.\n\nHowever, is it valid to fix target E=O=N/2? The graph labels determine which vertices are even/odd. We choose the actual sets as we assign layers, total even N/2, odd N/2. Fine.\n\nThen DP state can be (e, o, p, m, last_parity) but e,o determine remaining. Layer index parity maybe can be inferred from ne