eeds:\n  - bipartite edges between previous layer p and new c, with every new vertex non-isolated (coverage). No restriction on previous. B[p][c][x].\n  - edges within new layer: choose y from C(c,2): C(C(c,2), y).\nTotal increment x+y.\nPrevious layer's within edges already accounted.\n\nThen after adding layer, if all vertices used (e=N/2,o=N/2), we can stop. Otherwise continue next layer (which must be nonempty). At each layer, new vertices c>=1. This exactly counts all connected graphs rooted at 1? If every vertex except root has a neighbor in previous layer, graph is connected (each layer connected to previous), yes. Edges within layers arbitrary. BFS distances match. Good.\n\nNow DP dimensions and performance.\n\nN<=30, target E=O=N/2 <=15. This is key! e<=15, o<=15, not 30. State t up to30 but e,o each <=15. Last layer size p <=15 (since each color max15). m up to 435. Number states ~ e 16 * o 16 * p 16 * m 436 * parity2 ≈ 3.6M. Transitions c up to 15, and edge convolution over x/y up to p*c + C(c,2). Could be large but maybe manageable with optimized loops? Q= no. Need output all m.\n\nBut note many states maybe. Let's estimate naive: For each state, for c 1..remaining, for each x in B[p][c] (dense up to p*c <=225) and y in within options (up to C(c,2)<=105), combine => ~225*105=23k per c, too high. Need optimize convolutions via precomputed layer transition polynomial T[p][c][z] = coefficient of edges added between prev p and new c including within new c = convolution B[p][c] * C(C(c,2), y). Then transition m' = m+z. T polynomial length up to p*c + C(c,2) <=225+105=330. For each state and c, iterate over z nonzero and update. Still states maybe millions * 15 * hundreds too high.\n\nWe can reorganize DP as polynomial in edge count and use FFT? N small, but Python. Need better.\n\nMaybe use generating functions and only compute final coefficients via polynomial multiplication; DP over layers with polynomial in m. We can use Python big integers to represent po